{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In unsuccessful edits, examples that retain the Murcko scaffold have a significantly lower average target_delta than those that change the scaffold, indicating scaffold preservation reduces property change magnitude in failed attempts.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare mean target_delta between scaffold‐retained and scaffold‐changed groups in the unsuccessful dataset (train_neg.parquet) and test for a significant difference.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train_neg.parquet into a DataFrame.\n2. Drop rows where target_delta is null.\n3. Split into two subsets based on murcko_scaffold_retained == True vs False.\n4. Compute the mean and standard deviation of target_delta in each subset.\n5. Perform a two‐sample t‐test (or Welch’s t‐test if variances differ) to assess the difference in means.\n6. Visualize the distributions with side‐by‐side boxplots or violin plots.",  
      "deliverables":"- Mean and standard deviation of target_delta for scaffold‐retained and scaffold‐changed groups.\n- Test statistic, degrees of freedom, and p‐value from the t‐test.\n- Boxplot or violin plot comparing the two distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For successful edits, the change in molecular weight (MW_delta) differs significantly between tasks aiming to increase water solubility (task_id=101) and those aiming to increase drug-likeness (task_id=103), reflecting different structural modification strategies.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether MW_delta distributions differ between task 101 (water solubility) and task 103 (drug-likeness) in train.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null MW_delta.\n2. Filter rows where task_id is 101 or 103.\n3. Extract MW_delta arrays for each task.\n4. Check normality (e.g., Shapiro‐Wilk) and variance homogeneity (Levene’s test).\n5. Depending on assumptions, run either an independent two‐sample t‐test or a Mann–Whitney U test.\n6. Plot density curves or boxplots of MW_delta for both tasks for visual comparison.",  
      "deliverables":"- Sample size, mean, and variance of MW_delta for task 101 and task 103.\n- Results of normality and variance tests.\n- Test statistic and p‐value from the chosen statistical test.\n- Overlaid density plot or boxplot comparing MW_delta distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, longer natural language prompts (measured by word count) correlate positively with larger absolute changes in molecular weight (|MW_delta|), suggesting more detailed instructions yield greater structural modifications.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the relationship between prompt length and the magnitude of MW_delta in train.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null prompt or MW_delta.\n2. Compute a new column prompt_length = number of words in the prompt (split on whitespace).\n3. Compute abs_MW_delta = absolute value of MW_delta.\n4. Calculate Pearson and Spearman correlation coefficients between prompt_length and abs_MW_delta.\n5. Fit a simple linear regression model predicting abs_MW_delta from prompt_length and check significance of the slope.\n6. Generate a scatter plot with a regression line and confidence band.",  
      "deliverables":"- Pearson’s r, Spearman’s rho, and corresponding p‐values.\n- Regression slope, intercept, and p‐value.\n- Scatter plot of prompt_length vs abs_MW_delta with regression line."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Among successful edits, replace operations yield a higher mean target_delta than insert or delete operations, suggesting replacements are more effective at optimizing the target property.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare mean target_delta across action_type categories (insert, replace, delete) in train.parquet and test for overall differences.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null target_delta.\n2. Group data by action_type and compute group means and variances.\n3. Conduct a one‐way ANOVA to test for differences in means across the three action types.\n4. If ANOVA is significant, perform a post‐hoc Tukey HSD test to identify which pairs differ.\n5. Create a boxplot or bar chart with error bars showing mean target_delta by action_type.",  
      "deliverables":"- Group means and standard deviations of target_delta for each action_type.\n- ANOVA F‐statistic, degrees of freedom, and p‐value.\n- Post‐hoc pairwise comparison results.\n- Boxplot or bar chart of target_delta by action_type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Unsuccessful delete operations lead to a more negative average QED_delta compared to unsuccessful replace operations, indicating deletions are more detrimental to drug-likeness when the edit fails.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether mean QED_delta differs between delete and replace actions in the unsuccessful dataset (train_neg.parquet).",  
      "steps":"1. Load train_neg.parquet and drop rows where QED_delta is null.\n2. Filter to action_type 'delete' and 'replace'.\n3. Compute the mean and standard deviation of QED_delta for each group.\n4. Assess normality and equal variances; conduct either a two‐sample t‐test or Mann–Whitney U test.\n5. Visualize the comparison using violin or boxplots.",  
      "deliverables":"- Means and standard deviations of QED_delta for delete and replace.\n- Test statistic and p‐value from the selected test.\n- Violin or boxplot comparing QED_delta distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Successful edits for task_id=103 (increase drug-likeness) retain the Murcko scaffold at a higher rate than unsuccessful edits for the same task, suggesting scaffold preservation contributes to success in optimizing drug-likeness.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the proportion of murcko_scaffold_retained between successful (train.parquet) and unsuccessful (train_neg.parquet) examples for task_id=103.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet.\n2. Filter both datasets to rows where task_id == 103.\n3. For each subset, count the number of True vs False in murcko_scaffold_retained and compute proportions.\n4. Perform a chi‐square test of independence on the 2×2 contingency table.\n5. Create a grouped bar chart showing retention rates for successful vs unsuccessful examples.",  
      "deliverables":"- Contingency table and retention proportions for each group.\n- Chi‐square statistic, degrees of freedom, and p‐value.\n- Bar chart comparing scaffold retention rates."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Edits described with longer text (greater character length in the 'edit' field) are less likely to succeed, implying more complex edits reduce success probability.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Model the relationship between edit description length and edit success across both datasets.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet, add a binary column success = 1 for train, 0 for train_neg.\n2. Compute edit_length = number of characters in the edit description for all rows.\n3. Combine both datasets into one DataFrame.\n4. Fit a logistic regression model predicting success from edit_length, controlling for task_id (as categorical dummy variables).\n5. Examine the regression coefficient for edit_length and its p‐value.\n6. Plot observed success rate vs edit_length binned (e.g., deciles) with a trend line.",  
      "deliverables":"- Logistic regression summary table (coefficients, standard errors, p‐values).\n- Odds ratio for edit_length.\n- Binned success rate plot showing relationship with edit_length."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Examples with missing target_delta exhibit significantly smaller absolute changes in synthetic accessibility (|SA_delta|) than examples with available target_delta, suggesting missing target measurements correspond to minor edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the magnitude of SA_delta between rows with missing vs non‐missing target_delta in train.parquet.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet.\n2. Create two groups: rows where target_delta is null, and rows where target_delta is not null.\n3. Drop any rows with null SA_delta.\n4. Compute abs_SA_delta = absolute value of SA_delta for both groups.\n5. Calculate group means and variances of abs_SA_delta.\n6. Perform a two‐sample t‐test or Mann–Whitney U test to compare the groups.\n7. Visualize with side‐by‐side boxplots or density plots.",  
      "deliverables":"- Mean and variance of abs_SA_delta for missing vs non‐missing target_delta groups.\n- Test statistic and p‐value from the chosen test.\n- Boxplot or density plot comparing the distributions."  
    }  
  }  
]}

{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Longer natural‐language prompts reduce the success rate of molecular edits, as measured by the proportion of examples in train.parquet vs train_neg.parquet across prompt‐length deciles.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the relationship between prompt length and edit success rate.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet and concatenate into a single DataFrame with a binary label “success” (1 for train, 0 for train_neg).\n2. Compute prompt_length = number of words (or tokens) in the prompt column.\n3. Bin examples into deciles (10 bins) based on prompt_length.\n4. For each decile, compute the success rate = (# successful edits) / (total edits).\n5. Perform a Cochran–Armitage trend test or logistic regression of success ~ prompt_length decile to assess monotonic trend.\n6. Plot success rate vs. prompt_length decile with 95% confidence intervals.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Table of prompt_length deciles vs. success rate and counts. (b) Trend test statistic and p‐value. (c) Line plot of success rate across deciles with confidence bands."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Among successful edits (train.parquet), replace operations produce a higher average target_delta than insert or delete operations, controlling for task_id.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the effectiveness of action_type on target_delta within successful edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to non‐null target_delta.\n2. For each task_id, compute mean target_delta for each action_type (replace, insert, delete).\n3. Perform a two‐way ANOVA with factors task_id and action_type on target_delta.\n4. If interaction or main effects are significant, run post‐hoc pairwise comparisons (Tukey’s HSD) between action_types within each task.\n5. Visualize results: for each action_type, plot mean±SEM target_delta aggregated across tasks.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) ANOVA table (F‐statistics, p‐values) for action_type and task_id factors. (b) Post‐hoc comparison results. (c) Bar chart of mean target_delta by action_type with error bars."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Retaining the Murcko scaffold increases the probability of a successful edit, after adjusting for task_id.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the effect of murcko_scaffold_retained on edit success.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets, label success=1 for train.parquet and success=0 for train_neg.parquet.\n2. Remove rows with missing murcko_scaffold_retained.\n3. Encode murcko_scaffold_retained as binary (1/0) and task_id as categorical.\n4. Fit a logistic regression: success ~ murcko_scaffold_retained + C(task_id).\n5. Extract odds ratio for murcko_scaffold_retained, 95% CI, and p‐value.\n6. Validate model calibration (e.g., calibration curve) and compute AUC.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Logistic regression coefficients with odds ratios and CIs. (b) Model performance metrics (AUC, calibration plot). (c) Interpretation of scaffold retention effect."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Shorter edit descriptions (fewer characters in the edit column) correlate with larger improvements in target_delta among successful edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the relationship between edit description complexity and optimization success magnitude.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to non‐null target_delta.\n2. Compute edit_length = number of characters (or tokens) in the edit string.\n3. Calculate Pearson or Spearman correlation between edit_length and target_delta.\n4. Fit a linear regression target_delta ~ edit_length + C(task_id) to adjust for task effects.\n5. Plot a scatter plot with a regression line of target_delta vs. edit_length, colored by task cluster or aggregated trend.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Correlation coefficient and p‐value. (b) Regression summary (coefficients, R^2). (c) Scatter plot with regression overlay."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In QED‐optimization tasks (task_id=103), successful edits exhibit a negative correlation between QED_delta and SA_delta, indicating a trade‐off between drug‐likeness improvement and synthetic accessibility.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for a QED vs. SA trade‐off in successful QED‐focused edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to task_id==103 and non‐null QED_delta and SA_delta.\n2. Compute Pearson or Spearman correlation between QED_delta and SA_delta.\n3. Test significance of correlation.\n4. Fit a simple linear model SA_delta ~ QED_delta to estimate slope.\n5. Visualize with a scatter plot and regression line; annotate correlation coefficient.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Correlation coefficient and p‐value. (b) Regression slope with CI. (c) Scatter plot of SA_delta vs. QED_delta for task_id=103."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For water‐solubility optimization (task_id=101), edits that decrease molecular weight (negative MW_delta) achieve greater SA_delta improvements among successful edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Examine the relationship between MW_delta direction and SA_delta change in successful solubility edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet, filter to task_id==101 and non‐null MW_delta, SA_delta.\n2. Split examples into two groups: MW_delta < 0 and MW_delta >= 0.\n3. Compare mean SA_delta between the two groups using an unpaired t‐test or Mann–Whitney U test.\n4. Compute effect size (Cohen’s d) for SA_delta difference.\n5. Plot boxplots of SA_delta for the two MW_delta groups.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Group means and test statistic/p‐value. (b) Effect size. (c) Boxplot of SA_delta by MW_delta sign."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"A logistic regression model using MW_delta, SA_delta, QED_delta, action_type, murcko_scaffold_retained, and prompt_length predicts edit success with AUC>0.75.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Build and evaluate a multivariate classifier to predict edit success.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets, label success as before, and drop rows with any missing features.\n2. Derive prompt_length and encode action_type and murcko_scaffold_retained as dummy variables.\n3. Split data into train/test (e.g., 80/20 stratified by success).\n4. Standardize numeric features and fit a logistic regression (with L2 regularization) on the training set.\n5. Evaluate on the test set: compute ROC curve, AUC, accuracy, precision, recall.\n6. Perform 5‐fold cross‐validation to estimate generalization performance.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Test set AUC, accuracy, precision, recall. (b) ROC curve. (c) Cross‐validation mean AUC and standard deviation."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Tasks with longer average prompt lengths have lower overall success rates, indicating greater task complexity.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Identify whether prompt verbosity per task predicts difficulty (lower success rate).",  
      "steps":"1. For each dataset, compute prompt_length.\n2. For each task_id, compute mean prompt_length and success rate = (# successes)/(# successes + # failures).\n3. Across the 14 tasks, compute Pearson or Spearman correlation between mean prompt_length and success rate.\n4. Fit a linear regression success_rate ~ mean_prompt_length.\n5. Visualize with scatter plot of success rate vs. mean prompt_length and regression line.",  
      "deliverables":"(a) Correlation coefficient and p‐value. (b) Regression summary. (c) Scatter plot with fitted line."  
    }  
  }  
]}

{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, longer textual descriptions of the edit operation (greater edit string length) are associated with larger absolute improvements in the task-specific property (|target_delta|).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the complexity of the edit description (measured by edit text length or token count) correlates with the magnitude of the achieved property change in successful examples.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows where target_delta is null.  2. Compute two new columns: edit_length = number of characters in the 'edit' string; edit_tokens = number of whitespace-separated tokens.  3. Compute abs_target_delta = absolute value of target_delta.  4. Calculate Pearson and Spearman correlations between edit_length (and separately edit_tokens) and abs_target_delta.  5. Fit a simple linear regression model predicting abs_target_delta from edit_length (and another from edit_tokens).  6. Plot scatter plots with regression lines for both relationships.  7. Test statistical significance of correlations and regression coefficients.","deliverables":"(a) A table of summary statistics for edit_length, edit_tokens, and abs_target_delta.  (b) Correlation coefficients and p-values (Pearson and Spearman).  (c) Regression model summaries.  (d) Scatter plots with regression lines."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Delete operations have a lower probability of success compared to insert and replace across all optimization tasks.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and test differences in success rates among the three edit types (insert, replace, delete).",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet.  2. For each action_type, count successful and unsuccessful examples.  3. Build a contingency table of action_type vs success/failure.  4. Perform a chi-square test of independence to assess whether success is independent of action_type.  5. Compute success rates per action_type.  6. Visualize as a grouped bar chart: success_rate by action_type.","deliverables":"(a) Contingency table and proportions.  (b) Chi-square test statistic, degrees of freedom, and p-value.  (c) Table of success rates by action_type.  (d) Bar plot comparing success rates."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Among successful edits, increases in molecular weight (MW_delta) are inversely correlated with improvements in drug-likeness (QED_delta), indicating a MW–QED trade-off.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the relationship between MW_delta and QED_delta in successful examples to identify a trade-off pattern.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null MW_delta or QED_delta.  2. Compute Pearson and Spearman correlations between MW_delta and QED_delta.  3. Fit a bivariate linear regression model: QED_delta ~ MW_delta.  4. Create a scatter plot of MW_delta vs QED_delta with regression line and confidence interval.  5. Check for heteroskedasticity and nonlinearity.","deliverables":"(a) Correlation coefficients and p-values.  (b) Regression model summary (coefficients, R²).  (c) Diagnostic scatter plot with regression line."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Retaining the Murcko scaffold constrains the magnitude of molecular weight changes; edits that retain the scaffold exhibit lower variance in MW_delta than those that do not, in both successful and unsuccessful sets.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the variability of MW_delta between scaffold-retained and scaffold-modified edits in both datasets.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; drop rows with null MW_delta or murcko_scaffold_retained.  2. For each dataset, split into two groups by murcko_scaffold_retained (True/False).  3. Compute MW_delta variance and standard deviation for each group.  4. Perform Levene’s test for equality of variances between the two groups in each dataset.  5. Visualize distributions with side-by-side boxplots or violin plots of MW_delta by scaffold retention.","deliverables":"(a) Variance and SD tables for each group and dataset.  (b) Levene’s test statistics and p-values.  (c) Boxplots/violin plots of MW_delta distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Prompts containing explicit functional‐group keywords (e.g., 'hydroxyl', 'amine', 'halogen') lead to more pronounced improvements in synthetic accessibility (more negative SA_delta) than prompts without such keywords.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine if specific chemical‐group mentions in the natural language instructions predict larger SA improvements.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; drop rows with null SA_delta.  2. Define a keyword list: ['hydroxyl','amine','halogen','carboxyl','sulfhydryl'].  3. Create a binary feature has_keyword indicating if the prompt contains any keyword (case-insensitive).  4. Split the data into keyword and non-keyword groups.  5. Compare mean SA_delta between groups using a two-sample t-test (or nonparametric Mann–Whitney U test if non-normal).  6. Plot SA_delta distributions for both groups (boxplot/violin plot).","deliverables":"(a) Counts of examples with and without keywords.  (b) Mean and SD of SA_delta per group.  (c) Test statistic and p-value.  (d) Distribution plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Insert operations exhibit greater variability in molecular weight changes (MW_delta) than replace and delete operations in both successful and unsuccessful examples, reflecting more diverse fragment additions.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess and compare the spread of MW_delta across action types in both datasets.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; drop rows with null MW_delta or action_type.  2. For each dataset and each action_type, calculate MW_delta variance and interquartile range.  3. Use ANOVA or Kruskal–Wallis test to compare variances or distributions across action types for each dataset separately.  4. Visualize with side-by-side boxplots of MW_delta by action_type for successful and unsuccessful sets.","deliverables":"(a) Variance and IQR tables by action_type and dataset.  (b) ANOVA/Kruskal–Wallis test results and p-values.  (c) Comparative boxplots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For the water solubility optimization task (task_id=101), successful edits achieve a larger increase in the number of polar atoms (oxygen + nitrogen) than unsuccessful edits, reflecting effective polarity enhancement.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the change in count of polar atoms between input and output molecules for successful vs unsuccessful edits in task 101.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; filter rows where task_id==101.  2. For each row, parse input_smiles and output_smiles to count occurrences of 'O' and 'N'; compute delta_polar = (O+N)_output – (O+N)_input.  3. Compare mean delta_polar between successful and unsuccessful groups with a t-test or Mann–Whitney U test.  4. Plot both distributions side-by-side.","deliverables":"(a) Summary of mean and SD of delta_polar for both groups.  (b) Test statistic and p-value.  (c) Distribution plots (histogram or violin plot)."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Smaller changes in SMILES string length (|len(output_smiles) – len(input_smiles)|) are predictive of higher edit success likelihood across all tasks.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether the magnitude of SMILES length change is a predictor of edit success.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; drop rows with null SMILES.  2. Compute length_change = absolute difference in string length between output_smiles and input_smiles for each example.  3. Concatenate datasets and create a binary success label (1 for train.parquet, 0 for train_neg.parquet).  4. Fit a logistic regression model predicting success from length_change; optionally include task_id as categorical covariate.  5. Evaluate model performance via ROC curve and report AUC.  6. Plot success rate by binned length_change intervals.","deliverables":"(a) Logistic regression coefficients and p-values.  (b) ROC curve and AUC.  (c) Table of success rates by length_change bins.  (d) Plot of success rate vs length_change."  
    }  
  }  
]}

{"experiments":[
  {
    "hypothesis":"For the objective of increasing drug-likeness (task_id=103) in successful edits, molecules that retain their Murcko scaffold achieve a higher average target_delta than those that change their scaffold.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare mean target_delta between scaffold‐retained vs scaffold‐changed edits for task_id=103 in train.parquet.",
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter rows where task_id == 103 and target_delta is not null.\n2. Split the filtered data into two groups based on murcko_scaffold_retained (True vs False).\n3. Compute group means and standard deviations of target_delta.\n4. Conduct an independent two‐sample t-test (or Welch’s t-test if variances differ) to assess whether the mean target_delta differs between the two groups.\n5. Generate a bar chart with error bars showing mean ± standard error for each group.",
      "deliverables":"1. Table of group means, standard deviations, and sample sizes. 2. t-test statistic, degrees of freedom, and p-value. 3. Bar chart comparing mean target_delta for scaffold‐retained vs scaffold‐changed."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"In successful permeability optimization edits (task_id=105), insert operations produce larger increases in molecular weight (MW_delta) but also incur higher synthetic accessibility penalties (SA_delta) compared to delete operations.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Quantify and compare MW_delta and SA_delta for insert vs delete actions in task_id=105 successful edits.",
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter for task_id == 105, drop rows with null MW_delta or SA_delta.\n2. Filter rows where action_type is either 'insert' or 'delete'.\n3. For each action_type group, calculate mean and standard deviation of MW_delta and SA_delta.\n4. Perform two multivariate comparisons: (a) MW_delta by action_type via t-test; (b) SA_delta by action_type via t-test.\n5. Create side‐by‐side boxplots: one for MW_delta and one for SA_delta across action types.",
      "deliverables":"1. Summary statistics table for MW_delta and SA_delta by action_type. 2. t-test results for both comparisons (statistics and p-values). 3. Two boxplots illustrating the distributions."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Unsuccessful edits exhibit greater diversity of structural edits per prompt than successful edits, as measured by the number of unique edit strings for each prompt.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare edit‐string diversity (unique count per prompt) between train_parquet and train_neg_parquet.",
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet; drop rows with null 'edit'.\n2. For each dataset, group by prompt and count unique values of the 'edit' column. Record counts per prompt.\n3. For each dataset, compute the distribution (mean, median, IQR) of unique‐edit counts across prompts.\n4. Use a Mann–Whitney U test to compare prompt‐level unique‐edit count distributions between the two datasets.\n5. Plot side-by-side violin plots of unique‐edit counts for successful vs unsuccessful datasets.",
      "deliverables":"1. Distribution summary table of unique edit counts per prompt for both datasets. 2. Mann–Whitney U test statistic and p-value. 3. Violin plot comparing edit diversity."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"In water‐solubility optimization edits (task_id=101) within successful examples, edits that increase molecular weight by more than 50 Da are more likely to produce a negative change in QED (QED_delta < 0) than edits with MW_delta ≤ 50 Da.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether large MW increases (>50 Da) are associated with negative QED_delta for task_id=101 in train.parquet.",
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; filter for task_id == 101 and non‐null MW_delta and QED_delta.\n2. Create a binary feature high_MW_increase = (MW_delta > 50).\n3. For high_MW_increase True/False groups, compute counts of QED_delta < 0 and QED_delta ≥ 0.\n4. Build a 2×2 contingency table and perform chi‐squared or Fisher’s exact test to evaluate association.\n5. Display a grouped bar chart of proportions of negative‐QED outcomes by MW increase category.",
      "deliverables":"1. Contingency table with counts and proportions. 2. Test statistic and p‐value. 3. Bar chart of negative‐QED proportions for each MW_delta category."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"The correlation between synthetic accessibility change (SA_delta) and target property improvement (target_delta) is stronger in successful edits than in unsuccessful edits.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare the SA_delta vs target_delta correlation coefficients in train.parquet vs train_neg.parquet.",
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets; drop rows with null SA_delta or target_delta in each.\n2. For each dataset, compute Pearson and Spearman correlation coefficients between SA_delta and target_delta.\n3. Use Fisher’s Z‐transformation to test whether the two Pearson correlation coefficients differ significantly.\n4. Create scatterplots of SA_delta vs target_delta with overlaid regression lines for successful and unsuccessful datasets side by side.\n5. Annotate each plot with its correlation coefficient and p-value.",
      "deliverables":"1. Table of Pearson and Spearman correlations and sample sizes for each dataset. 2. Z‐test statistic and p-value comparing correlations. 3. Paired scatterplots with regression lines."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Within unsuccessful edits, the probability of retaining the Murcko scaffold differs significantly across the three action types (insert, replace, delete).",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether murcko_scaffold_retained rates vary by action_type in train_neg.parquet.",
